{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS3LKdskWYkPo2fn3utK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found three causes and fixed two with confidence. The third, the 400 on the authorize URL, is my best guess. I checked only that `server.py` parses. I didn't run the server or test against the real Intervals.icu, and I made no commits.\n\n1. **Connect button fails for everyone.** The button was a plain link, and a browser navigation can't send the `Authorization: Bearer` header, so the server returned 401. The button now fetches `/api/intervals/auth/init` through the app's `fetch` wrapper, which adds the header. The endpoint returns `{\"url\": ...}` and the page navigates to that URL.\n2. **400 `invalid_request` on the authorize URL.** This started when calendar sync added `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` to the scopes. I trimmed the scopes in `config.py` to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. The scope string is a guess: Intervals.icu's error doesn't name the bad parameter, and `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ` is the only set known to have worked. If it still returns 400, try scope sets one at a time against the authorize URL.\n3. **\"Reconnect\" after an hour.** The Intervals.icu token response has no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token`, so its tokens appear to be long-lived. The code invented a 3600-second lifetime and then expired the connection because it had no refresh token. A token with no expiry info is now treated as non-expiring, and the refresher skips it.\n\nThe causes are written up in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2867,"cache_read_input_tokens":28500,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2867},"output_tokens":538,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":nul